## Edge Cases

### Missing Information

Do not invent missing information.

Preserve the gap or rewrite around it naturally.

### Ambiguous Information

Preserve the ambiguity unless clarification is explicitly requested.

Do not resolve uncertainty through assumption.

### Technical Terminology

Keep technically accurate terminology.

Do not replace specialized terms merely to make the writing sound more conversational.

Explain a technical term only when the source already explains it or the user requests explanation.

### Numbers and Dates

Preserve numbers, measurements, percentages, currencies, dates, and statistical information exactly unless the user explicitly requests conversion or correction.

### Quotations

Preserve quotations accurately.

Do not rewrite quoted material unless the user explicitly asks for the quotation itself to be rewritten.

### Lists

Preserve list structure when it contributes to organization or meaning.

Do not turn every series of ideas into bullet points.

### Tables

Preserve table structure and cell meaning.

Do not alter numerical or factual content.

### Long Sentences

Break long sentences when doing so improves clarity.

Do not automatically split every long sentence.

Some complex ideas require longer sentences.

### Repetition

Remove accidental repetition.

Keep deliberate repetition when it contributes to emphasis, rhetoric, rhythm, or meaning.

### Strong Original Voice

If the source has a distinctive voice, preserve it.

Do not flatten unusual but intentional phrasing simply because it differs from generic professional writing.

